Acute Bronchitis
Respiratory System
Persistent cough, Purulent/clear sputum, Normal chest X-ray

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-002
Reason for Visit: Unrelenting, painful cough with purulent sputum preventing sleep and causing moderate retrosternal pain, necessitating urgent symptom management and assessment for lower respiratory tract infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated severe paroxysmal coughing leading to suspected exhaustion and chest wall pain. Urgent assessment was required to exclude pneumonia and initiate symptomatic control prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I have been coughing so hard for a week that my chest physically aches and I am exhausted.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms commenced 7 days ago following an upper respiratory tract infection. Over the past 72 hours, the persistent cough has intensified, producing purulent/clear sputum. The patient describes retrosternal chest wall pain due to vigorous coughing and mild dyspnea on exertion. There is a low-grade fever but no major rigors. The patient reports the coughing is worse at night, causing significant sleep deprivation.
Functional Status: Independent, but fatigued due to disrupted sleep.
Social/Safety Concerns: None identified; supportive spouse present.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/78 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 37.6 C | RR: 18 breaths/min | SpO2: 97% on room air | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Fatigued appearance, frequent coughing fits during the interview, no severe respiratory distress.
Physical Exam: Auscultation reveals mild wheezing and rhonchi that clear with coughing. No signs of focal consolidation, egophony, or tactile fremitus are present. Cardiovascular exam is unremarkable.
Environmental Observations: Dry indoor air; no obvious environmental triggers.
Medication Reconciliation: Over-the-counter cough suppressants utilized with no significant effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Bronchitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of high fever, focal crepitations, or signs of consolidation.

Pertussis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of classic inspiratory whoop or post-tussive emesis, and patient is fully vaccinated.

Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of purulent sputum and preceding viral URI symptoms.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is presenting with acute bronchitis characterized by a persistent cough, purulent sputum, and retrosternal pain secondary to coughing efforts. The absence of focal signs of consolidation and normal oxygen saturations make pneumonia unlikely. However, the severity of the paroxysmal coughing is causing significant distress, sleep deprivation, and chest pain. Waiting until the next in-hours period for assessment would leave the patient in unmanaged pain and distress. An urgent after-hours assessment was justified to confidently rule out a more serious lower respiratory tract infection, avoid unnecessary emergency department presentation, and prescribe appropriate short-term symptomatic relief.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a short course of antitussives for nighttime use to facilitate sleep. Advised simple analgesia (paracetamol/ibuprofen) for chest wall pain.
Positive Behavioral Change: Keep a glass of water by the bed and take small sips during coughing fits; this action moistens the irritated pharynx, directly leading to a reduction in the tickle reflex and fewer coughing spasms.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Humidifier recommended for the bedroom.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days if symptoms do not improve or if fever spikes.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Seek immediate medical attention if breathing becomes difficult at rest, if coughing up blood occurs, or if a high fever over 39 C develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis relies on current clinical findings; chest X-ray may be required if symptoms persist to confirm normal findings.
Clinical Course: The cough in acute bronchitis can persist for 1 to 3 weeks. Symptomatic improvement is expected, but the cough will be the last symptom to resolve.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return for reassessment if you develop shortness of breath, sharp chest pain when taking a breath, or if you begin coughing up blood.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Antibiotics are generally ineffective for acute bronchitis, which is typically viral. Treatment focuses on soothing the airways and managing pain.
Actionable Steps: Take the prescribed pain relief regularly rather than waiting for the pain to become severe; staying ahead of the pain allows you to take deep breaths and clear sputum effectively.

